## ABSTRACT
During thecatalyticprocess,themicroenviromentandsurfaceareaofthecatalystwillafectthecatalyticperformance.Hence, anassistedorganiclinkercoatedmetal-organicframework(MOF)hasbeenaplied,toformNi/HNC(HNCrepresents hollow nanocage) for electrocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. Remarkably, Ni/HNC achieves superb activity with high Faradaic efficiency (FE） of $97.2\%$ at $0.7\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) towards ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion to CO. In contrast to Ni/NPC (afforded from the naked MOF), the Ni/HNC displays higher FE and selectivity on CO rather than ${\sf H}_{2}$ ， owing to the large nanocage which extraordinarily facilitates ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ enrichment and the active sites easily accessible. This work provides a general and feasible route to construct high-efficient electrochemical ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(E C\mathrm{-}C O_{2}R R)$ catalysts via post-modified MOFs.
## KEYWORDS
post-modification, atomically dispersed sites, hollow nanocage, electrocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction
## Introduction
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is a greenhouse gas with adverse effects, causing an increase in global average temperature, changes in climate, ocean acidification, and the destruction of carbon cycle in nature [1-3]. The extensive consumption of fossil fuels has caused the increasing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emission, bringing urgent and terrible climate problems [4-6]. The most promising way to mitigate the negative effects of excessive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions is to convert them into valueadded chemicals and fuels, achieving carbon neutrality. As we know from the previous reports [7-l2], carbon dioxide can be reduced through thermal catalysis [13-15], photocatalysis [16-18], and electrocatalysis [19-24] to generate a variety of products. Among these approaches, the photochemical and electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction ( $\mathrm{PC-CO_{2}R R}$ and $\operatorname{EC-CO}_{2}\operatorname{RR},$ toproduce chemicals and fuels have attracted a great deal of attention due to the mild reaction conditions and considerable energy efficiency. Both of them can mitigate the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emission and supply sustainable energy to the society. For example, the conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into CO is a fairly economic and feasible route, because CO can be used as a fundamental chemical feedstock in the following chemicals manufacturing, medicine, and so on [25].
As we all have known, a desirable electrocatalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion should be highly active, selective, and stable towards targeted products at the low overpotential. The atomically dispersed metal (ADM) catalysts have emerged and flourished in the field of catalysis due to their ultrahigh atom utilization efficiency and high surface energy results in their superb catalytic activity, attracting more and more attention in recent years [26-28]. Zeolitic-imidazolate-frameworks (ZIFs) [29], as a class of crystalline porous materials with stable and large frameworks which are beneficial for substrate enrichment, have been more and more applied to fabricate heterogeneous catalysts [30,31]. However, how to make more active sites exposed to the substrate still puzzles the materials scientists.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Catalysts synthesis
Synthesis of Zn-MOF. 3.72 g $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ and 4.11 g
2-methylimidazole were dissolved in $100~\mathrm{\mL}$ anhydrous methanol, respectively, and dissolved by ultrasound. Then the solution containing 2-methylimidazole was added to the solution containing $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O},$ followed by ultrasonic treatment, and finally  stood for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The precipitate  was collected  by centrifugation and washed with methanol several times and dried in vacuum at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight. After grinding, white powder was obtained.
Synthesis of MOF@DA. 300 mg Zn-MOF was dispersed in a mixed solution of $94~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, $36~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous ethanol, and $2\ \mathrm{mL}$ ammonia water. And $40~\mathrm{\mg}$ dopamine hydrochloride was dissolved in a mixed solution of $6~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water and $4~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous ethanol. After stirring for $0.5\quad\mathrm{h},$ respectively, the solution containing dopamine hydrochloride was added to the solution containing MOF, followed by stirring for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ together. The precipitate was collected by centrifugation and washed with the mixed solution of ethanol and deionized water several times and dried in vacuum at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight. After grinding, gray brown powder was obtained.
Synthesis of HNC. The powder of $\mathrm{MOF@DA}$ was placed in a tube furnace. Then the sample was heat-treated in Ar atmosphere at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a heating rate of $5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and then naturally cooled to room temperature. Black powder could be obtained.
Synthesis of Ni/HNC. The resultant black HNC was dispersed in a mixed solution of $6\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water and $4\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous ethanol. Then $15\mathrm{mgNiPc}$ was added to the above solution.After stirring at room temperature for $^{12\mathrm{h},}$ the precipitate was collected by centrifugation and washed with the mixed solution of ethanol and deionized water several times and dried in vacuum at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight. The resultant powder was placed in a tube furnace. Then the sample was heat-treated in Ar atmosphere at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ with a heating rate of $5~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and then naturally cooled to room temperature. The final black powder was the target catalyst.
Synthesis of Ni/HNC $(\mathbf{NO}_{3}^{-})$ and Ni/HNC (Cl-). The synthesis method was the same as that of Ni/HNC. The only difference was that the NiPc was replaced by $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ${\mathrm{NiCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O};$ respectively.
Synthesis of Ni/NPC. $300~\mathrm{mg~MOF}$ was dispersed in a mixed solution of deionized water and $15\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{NiPc}$ followed by stirring for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The precipitate was collected by centrifugation and washed with the mixed solution of ethanol and deionized water several times and dried in vacuum at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight. The resultant powder was placed in a tube furnace. Then the sample was heat-treated in Ar atmosphere at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ with a heating rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and then naturally cooled to room temperature.
## 2.2 Characterization
The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded using a Bruker D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer with Cu $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=1.54\mathrm{\AA}$ ). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was carried out by a Hitachi 8l0o (scanning electron microscopy at an acceleration voltage of $5~\mathrm{kV}\cdot$ ). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained by Hitachi HT7700 (accelerating voltage of $100\mathrm{kV}\cdotp$ ). The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were carried out on ESCALAB 250. The spherical aberration-corrected high-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) images were acquired on a Japan JEMARM200F instrument at $200\mathrm{keV}.$ The Raman spectrogram of the samples was recorded on a Britain Renishaw in Via equipped with laser whose excitation wavelength is $633~\mathrm{nm}$ XPS measurements were conducted by utilizing a thermo ESCALAB 250 highperformance electron spectrometer using monochromatized Al $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ 0 $h\nu=1486.6\mathrm{~eV},$ ）as the excitation source. The content of Ni atoms in the catalysts was determined by an Optima $7300~\mathrm{DV}$ inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometer (ICPAES). $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption measurements were conducted using a Micromeritics ASAP 2460 system. The X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) spectra data (Ni K-edge) were recorded at 1W1B station in Beijing Synchrotron Radiation Facility (BSRF, electron storage ring operated at $2.5\mathrm{\GeV}$ with a maximum current of $250\mathrm{mA}$ .The data were collected in fluorescence excitation mode using a Lytle detector. All solid samples were pelletized as disks of $13\mathrm{mm}$ diameter with $1\mathrm{mm}$ thickness using graphite powder as a binder and measured at room temperature.
## 2.3 Electrochemical measurements
All electrochemical experiments were carried out in a standard three-electrode system using the electrochemical workstation (CHI660E). Platinum rod and $\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}$ were used as counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively, and modified carbon paper was used as work electrode. The electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ performance was carried out in an airtight electrochemical H-type cell containing $0.5\mathrm{M}\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ solution, in which the anode and cathode of electrolytic cell were separated by a Nafion $^{\infty}117$ proton exchange membrane. The catalyst-modified carbon paper electrode (denoted as CPE, ${1}\mathrm{cm}\times2\mathrm{cm},$ was prepared as follows. $5\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst was ground to form the uniform black powder. This black powder was dispersed into 0.l mL $1\%$ Nafion solution with $0.4~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water and sonication for $30\ \mathrm{\min}$ to form uniform catalyst ink. Then, the ink was dropped directly onto the surface of carbon paper $(1\ \mathrm{cm}\times1\ \mathrm{cm})$ ） with a catalyst loading density of $\sim1\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and dried at ambient temperature. The polarization curves were performed by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) mode at a scan rate of $10\ \mathrm{\mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . In this experiment, polarization curves were recorded successively in Ar-saturated and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ solution.
## 2.4 FE calculations
$\mathrm{FE}=Q_{\mathrm{CO}}/Q_{\mathrm{total}}=(n\times Z\times F)/Q_{\mathrm{total}}$ $Q_{\mathrm{total}}\mathrm{:}$ the charge passed, $\mathrm{C};$ $Q_{\mathrm{CO}}$ : the charge used for the reduction of CO, $\mathrm{C};$ $n\mathrm{:}$ :the number of moles for CO (measured by gas   
chromatography(GC)), mol; $Z\colon$ the number of electrons transferred, which is 2 for CO; F: Faradaic constant, $96{,}485\mathrm{C{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$
## 2.5 Computational methods
Electronic structure calculations were performed using the QE package with the generalized gradient approximation (GGA） of Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE） to the exchange-correlation functional [34-36]. The electronic-ion interactions were considered in the form of the projector-augmented-wave (PAW) method. The kinetic cutoff energy for a plane-wave function was $35\mathrm{Ry}$ . The pristine graphene layer was used as a $p(5\times5)$ supercell including $50~\mathrm{C}$ atoms. Six C atoms were replaced by a planar pyridine-like $\mathrm{NiN_{4}}$ center. The $2\times2$ Monkhorst-Pack $k$ -point sampling was used. All atoms were relaxed during geometry optimizations. The Gibbs reaction free energy was calculated by $\Delta G=\Delta E+\Delta\mathrm{ZPE}-T\Delta S,$ where $E$ is the total electronic energy of species, ZPE is the zero-point energy, S is the entropy, and $T$ is $300~\mathrm{K}.$ The adsorption energy was defined as: $E_{\mathrm{ad}}=E(\mathrm{ad/surf})\textrm{-}$ $E(\mathrm{ad})-E(\mathrm{surf}).$ where $E(\mathrm{ad/surf})$ 。 $E(\mathrm{ad})$ and $E(\mathrm{surf})$ are the total energies of the adsorbate binding to surface, free adsorbate in vacuum, and the clean surface, respectively. The free energy of $1/2\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas could replace that of $\mathrm{H^{+}+e^{-}}$ for the presence of the equilibrium of $\mathrm{H}^{+}\ +\ \mathrm{e}^{-}\ \rightarrow\ 1/2\mathrm{H}_{2},$ under standard hydrogen electrode condition. Water effects were considered by the adsorption of two water molecules on the reactive sites.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Structural and morphological characterizations of Ni/HNC
As illustrated in Fig.1, the preparation can be simply divided into three steps. Firstly, the as-synthesized MOF was dispersed, and then the DA solution was dropped to form $\mathrm{MOF@DA}$ Secondly, the mixture was dried and pyrolyzed at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (close to the boiling point of zinc) to obtain HNC. Finally, the solution with metal salts was impregnated into the hollow nanocages, and then the nickel species can be proposed to be anchored into the HNC with the nitrogen sites, followed by calcining again to form Ni/HNC. Obviously, this is different from the previously reports such as commonly used MOFs as precursor [37,38], in this work, the layer of DA can provide amounts of N sites, which generates highloading single atoms sites as confirmed by the consistency of ICP results, maximizing the density of active sites, and more, the assisted DA can be pyrolyzed to form carbon framework to obtain HNC. The SEM (Fig. S3 in the ESM) images show that $\mathrm{MOF@DA}$ possesses a significantly different rough surface compared to MOF precursors (Fig. S2 in the ESM), with a certain increase from $\sim400$ to $\sim500\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter,which indicates that dopamine has been successfully wrapped on the surface of MOF. Benefitting from this special structure, the subsequently added nickel salt can coordinate well with nitrogen groups in dopamine, which can prevent the metal from aggregation during the following calcination. As expected, there is no obvious particles and clusters can be detected (Figs. 2(b) and 2(c)). To study the loading of Ni sites, the aberration corrected HAADF-STEM (AC-HAADF-STEM) images were measured. Amounts of individual Ni sites could be detected as bright spots (Figs. 2(d) and 2(e)), suggesting the highlyloading isolated distribution of Ni sites in the hollow nanocages. The powder XRD (PXRD) patterns also show no other peaks except a broad peak located around $26^{\circ}{}_{;}$ which is assigned to the (002) plane of graphitic carbon matrix (Fig.S1 in the ESM). This result furtherly proves the nickel species are atomically dispersed in the nanocages. Furthermore, the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) and Raman spectra were also performed to characterize the as-synthesized catalysts, manifesting that the catalyst maintains the characteristic of hollow nanocages with the BET surface of $1035~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ，which is larger than that of Ni/NPC (Fig.S12 in the ESM). Most importantly, inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) reveals the content of Ni $(\sim11~\mathrm{wt.\%})$ ),which is superior to previous reported atomically dispersed catalysts fabricated by pyrolysis of MOFs precursor and Ni/NPC. Furthermore, the hollow structure can make most of the metal sites easily accessible, benefitting for the catalytic process which enhances the use of the active sites.
## 3.2 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ performance in H-cell
To evaluate the performance of the as-synthesized catalysts, the selectivity and reaction rate towards electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ were implemented in H-type cell with a standard three-electrode system and filled with a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ $(0.5\mathrm{~M})$ solution by controlled potential electrolysis (the experimental details can be found in the ESM). The obtained gaseous products were monitored by the GC, and no liquid products were detected by the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy (Fig.S14 in the ESM). Only gas products of CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were detected for all of the as-synthesized catalysts. As disclosed by the LSV curves (Fig. 4(a)),Ni/HNC has the lowest onset potential and the highest current densities among various samples, significantly surpassing those of the ${\mathrm{Ni/HNC}}$ $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\right)$ ，Ni/HNC $\left(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\right)$ ，and Ni/NPC catalysts. Furthermore, Ni/HNC shows much higher current density in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated solution than that of in Ar-saturated electrolyte, uncovering the high electrochemical activity of Ni/HNC (inset of Fig. 4(a)). Meanwhile, the catalytic activity of all the catalysts is evaluated and the corresponding FE values are calculated in the potential range from $-0.5$ to $-1.0\mathrm{V.Ni/HNC}$ has higher $\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}}$ value in the wide range of potential (from $-0.5$ to $-1.0\mathrm{V})$ than other catalysts, even reaching up to maximum value of $97.2\%$ at $0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig.4(b)). For comparison, the CO partial current densities $\mathrm{(CD_{CO})}$ were tested at different applied potential, displaying that the $\mathrm{CD}_{\mathrm{CO}}$ of Ni/HNC ( $22.5\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ Vs. RHE) is obviously higher than that of Ni/HNC $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}{}^{-}\right)$ $(8.6\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE),Ni/HNC(Cl) $\mathrm{12.7\mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ at $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE), and Ni/NPC( $\mathrm{(16.1\mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ at $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ vs.RHE), which is attributed to its highest FE in all the potential ranges. However, the reduction occurs at $-0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. RHE,Fig. $4(\mathrm{c})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ， Ni/HNC displays a slightly lower partial current density may be caused by a small current fluctuation during the electrolysis process. The higher catalytic performance of Ni/HNC indicates the difference in the anion precursors. All the results may be attributed to Ni/HNC that exhibits the largest BET surface area and pore volume $(0.265\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ ， benefitting for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ enrichment and the product release, and the most defects, which can be proved by Raman spectra (Fig.S8 in the ESM),in the carbon matrix of Ni/HNC result in fast electron transformation during the catalysis process.
To uncover the reaction kinetics of different sites in the electrocatalytic process, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and Tafel plots were carried out. As revealed by the Nyquist plots (Fig.4(d)), the charge-transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ of Ni/HNC is significantly lower than that of Ni/HNC $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\right.$ ),Ni/HNC $\left(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\right)$ ， and Ni/NPC, implying the faster charge-transfer rate for Ni/HNC catalyst. As shown in the Tafel slope (Fig.4(e)), Ni/HNC displays lower Tafel slope of $201.75\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ than those of ${\mathrm{Ni/HNC}}$ $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}{}^{-}\right)$ 1 $295.45\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ ),Ni/HNC $\left(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\right)$ $(270.68\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ ，and Ni/NPC( $249.37\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}},$ , indicating a faster kinetics of Ni/HNC for ${\mathrm{EC-CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}},$ which allows the electron to transfer to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ more easily from the surface of catalyst. Compared with Ni/HNC $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}{}^{-}\right)$ ，Ni/HNC $(\mathrm{Cl^{-}})$ ，and Ni/NPC, the excellent $_\mathrm{EC-CO_{2}R R}$ performance of Ni/HNC reveals the great superiority of the atomically dispersed metal sites and the introduced acid ions affect the intrinsic microstructure of metal ions, therefore accelerating charge transfer and mass transformation. Moreover, Ni/HNC also shows excellent durability for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion to CO, giving almost no decrease of $\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}}$ and current density after $10\mathrm{{h}}$ reaction (Fig.4(f)).
more accessible active sites, better substrate gathering, and faster electron transform. In contrast to Ni/HNC $(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-})$ and $\mathrm{{Ni/HNC}}$ $(\mathrm{Cl^{-}})$ ,the enough N-dopant of Ni/HNC allows the metal sites stable, resulting in highly-loading of active sites dispersed on HNC which leads to excellent activity. This work not only presents a feasible and facile strategic direction to design highly-loading atomically dispersed catalyst, but for the first time considers that the different anions were used in the synthesis of materials,which further affects the electrochemical performance.
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, a hollow nanocage has been fabricated via postmodification of MOF to afford Ni/HNC with a highly-loading atomically dispersed Ni sites, which possesses the most nitrogen doped, larger pore size, and most carbon defects, exhibiting amazing performance on the EC $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ towards CO conversion with the highest Faradaic efficiency of $97.2\%$ Compared with Ni/NPC, the superb performance may be attributed to the large nanocage and highly-loading single atoms make Ni/HNC possess
